      "textEn": "164\n\nJanasyatrahachyuta satsamagamah. In the company of the wise, there is always goodness.\nIn you, the supreme intellect is born. 5. Meaning of the word\n\nBhava—of existence; Apavarga—completion; Bhramatah—wandering; Yada—when; Bhaveth—happens; Janasya—for humans; Tahi—that time; Achyuta—Oh Achyuta; Sat—of the saints; Samagama—association; Sat-Sangama—association with the saints; Yarhi—when; Tada—then; Eva—only; Sat—of the saints; Gataau—the goal, which; Par—of the superior (of the world); Avar—likewise, inferior (of the work);\nIsha—in God; Tvayi—to you; Jayate—are born; Matiha—devotion. Hey Achyuta, when the physical life of the wandering soul (jiva) ends, then he\ncan attain the company of your devotees. And when he associates with them, then the devotees'\ngoals and all the causes and the master of their effects, devotion arises towards you in that.\nMeaning: Acharya Jiva Goswami and Vishwanath Chakravarti are unanimous on this point that although here\nit is said that when physical life ends, a person attains the company of devotees\nbut in reality, only through the company of devotees of God can a person cross the ocean of life. For this apparent\ndiscrepancy, Srila Jiva Goswami quotes Kaviprakasha (.15)— 'Karyakaranayoshcha'\n'Paurvaparyavyavartyo vijnayetishayoktih syat'—That statement in which the logical order of cause and effect\nis reversed is called hyperbole. On this statement, there is a commentary by Srila Jiva Goswami—'For the sake of quick action, to express the prior mention of the task—To express the quick action of a cause, the prior fruit of the cause can be emphasized. In this context, Srila Vishwanath Chakravarti points out that by the graceful association of devotees of God,\nour resolve to be absorbed in Krishna consciousness becomes possible. And the Acharyas agree with Srila Jeeva Goswami\nthat there is hyperbole in this verse.\nBy whose grace, the Lord has done this.\nThe king's departure from the association, by ch