      "text": "167\n\nवरों के द्वारा तुम्हें प्रलोभित करना चाहा किन्तु तुम्हारा मन भौतिक इच्छाओं के वशीभूत नहीं\nहुआ।\n\nप्रलोभितो वरैर्यत्त्वमप्रमादाय विद्धि तत् ।\nन धीरेकान्तभक्तानामाशीर्भिर्भिद्यते क्वचित् ॥ ५९ ॥\n\nशब्दार्थ\n\nप्रलोभितः — प्रलोभन में आये; वरैः — वरों से; यत् — जो तथ्य; त्वम् — तुम; अप्रमादाय — मोह से मुक्त होने के लिए; विद्धि — जानो; तत् — वह; न — नहीं; धीः — बुद्धि; एकान्त — एकमात्र; भक्तानाम् — भक्तों के; आशीर्भिः — आशीर्वादों से; भिद्यते — विपथ होती है, भटकती है; क्वचित् — कभी।\n\nयह जान लो कि मैं यह सिद्ध करने के लिए तुम्हें वरों से प्रलोभन दे रहा था कि तुम धोखा\nनहीं खा सकते। मेरे शुद्ध भक्त की बुद्धि कभी भी भौतिक आशीर्वादों से विपथ नहीं होती।\n\nयुञ्जानानामभक्तानां प्राणायामादिभिर्मनः ।\nअक्षीणवासनं राजन्हृष्यते पुनरुत्थितम् ॥ ६० ॥\n\nशब्दार्थ\n\nयुञ्जानानाम्—लगे रहने वाले का; अभक्तानाम्—अभक्तों का; प्राणायाम—प्राणायाम से; आदिभिः—इत्यादि से; मनः—मन;\nअक्षीण—निर्मूल नहीं हुई; वासनम्—वासना के अन्तिम अवशेष; राजन्—हे राजन् (मुचुकुन्द); दृश्यते—देखी जाती हैं;\nपुनः—फिर; उत्थितम्—जगती हुई (इन्द्रिय तृप्ति के विचारों के प्रति)।\n\nऐसे अभक्तगण जो प्राणायाम जैसे अभ्यासों में लगते हैं उनके मन भौतिक इच्छाओं से कभी\nविमल नहीं होते। इस तरह हे राजन्, उनके मन में भौतिक इच्छाएँ पुनः उठती हुई देखी गई हैं।\n\nविचरस्व महीं कामं मय्यावेशितमानसः ।\nअस्त्वेवं नित्यदा तुभ्यं भक्तिर्मय्यनपायिनी ॥ ६१ ॥\n\nशब्दार्थ\n\nविचरस्व—भ्रमण करो; महीम्—इस पृथ्वी पर; कामम्—इच्छानुसार; मयि—मुझमें; आवेशित—स्थिर; मानसः—तुम्हारा मन;\nअस्तु—होए; एवम्—इस प्रकार; नित्यदा—सदैव; तुभ्यम्—तुम्हारे लिए; भक्तिः—भक्ति; मयि—मुझमें; अनपायिनी—\nअविचल ।\n\nअपना मन मुझमें स्थिर करके तुम इच्छानुसार इस पृथ्वी पर विचरण करो। मुझमें तुम्हारी\nऐसी अविचल भक्ति सदैव बनी रहे।\n\nक्षात्रधर्मस्थितो जन्तून्न्यवधीर्मृगयादिभिः ।\nसमाहितस्तत्तपसा जह्यघं मदुपाश्रितः ॥ ६२ ॥",
      "textEn": "167\n\nBy tempting you, they wanted to, but your mind was not subdued by material desires. The one who is tempted, know that he is not trustworthy. Not by slow-witted devotees is it possible to understand. 59. Meaning of the word\n\nLured - come into temptation; Varaih - by boons; Yat - that fact; Tvam - you; Apramaada - to be free from delusion; Viddhi - know; Tat - that; Na - not; Dheehi - intellect; Ekanta - alone; Bhaktanam - of devotees; Ashirbhih - with blessings; Bhidyate - becomes, wanders; Kvachit - sometimes. Know that I was tempting you with gifts to prove that you cannot be deceived.\nYou cannot be deceived. The intellect of my pure devotee is never ruined by material blessings. Of those who are devoted to the senses, the mind is controlled by pranayama and other practices.\nAkshinavasanam rajan hrushyate punarutthanam. 60. Meaning of the word\n\nYunjanaam—those who are engaged; Abhaktaam—devotees; Pranayama—from Pranayama; Adibhishah—from etc.; Manah—mind;\nAkshina—not destroyed; Vasanam—the last remains of desire; Rajan—Oh King (Muchukunda); Drishyate—are seen;\nPunah—Again; Uththitam—having risen (to worldly pleasures); Vicharan—to contemplate; Indriya—of the senses; Trupti—satisfaction;\nVicharan—to contemplate; Vicharan—to contemplate; Vicharan—to contemplate; Vicharan—to contemplate; Vicharan—to contemplate; Vicharan—to contemplate; Vicharan—to contemplate; Vicharan—to contemplate; Vicharan—to contemplate; Vicharan—to contemplate; Vicharan—to contemplate; Vicharan—to contemplate Such devotees who engage in practices like pranayama, their minds are never\npure from material desires. Thus, O King, material desires are seen to arise again in their minds. Vicharasva mahi kamam mayaveshitamanasah. Thus, always, with devotion, I am yours, the means to liberation. 61. Meaning of the word\n\nVicharasva—to wander; Mahim—on this earth; Kamam—desire; Mayi—in me; Aveshita—stable; Manasah—your mind;\nAstu—be; Evam—in this way; Nityada—always; Tubhyam—for you; Bhakti—devotion; Mayi—in me; Anapayini—\nUnshakeable. Keep your mind fixed on me and wander on this earth as you please.\nMay your\nsuch unwavering devotion always remain in me. The people who are situated in the field of kshatra, also hunt animals. Controlled by the heat of the burning sensation, dependent on me. 62."
