      "textEn": "17\n\nLike a rich, abundant, and fearful one. Thousands and miles of palasha trees. 8 ”. Meaning of the word\n\nVihay—to leave; vittam—wealth; prabhram—sufficient; abitau—in reality, without fear; bhiru—like timid ones; bhit-vat—frightened; pada-bhyam—with feet; padma—of lotuses; palashabhyam—like petals; cheltu-hai—went away; bahu-yojanam—many yojanas (one yojana is a distance of about eight miles). Leaving behind abundant wealth, fearlessly, but pretending to be afraid, they walked many yojanas with their lotus-like feet. Seeing them flee, the Magadha laughed loudly. Anvadhavadrathanikairisyoraprathamanvit. 9. Meaning of the word\n\nPalayamanau—running; Tau—both of them; Drishtva—seeing; Magadha—Jarasandha; Prahasan—laughing loudly; Bali—strong;\nAnvadhavat—running behind; Ratha—chariot; Anikaih—with the soldiers; Ishayoh—of both lords; Apramana-vit—\nUnaware of the field of work.\nSeeing them both running, the mighty Jarasandha began to make a commotion, and taking the charioteers and\nfoot soldiers along, he began to chase them. He could not understand the high position of both lords.\nHe could not understand. Distant, distant, the mountain ranges, the mountain ranges, the mountain ranges. The rain-bearing cloud, where it rains daily. Meaning of the word:\nPradrutya—running with full speed; Dooram—a considerable distance; Sansrantau—tired; Tungam—very high; Aaruhithaam—climbed; Girim—mountain;\nPar—over; Pravarshana-akhyam—named Pravarshana; Bhagavan—Indra; Nityada—always; Yatra—where; Varshati—it rains..\nTired of running for a long distance, both the lords, looking crushed, climbed on a high mountain called Pravarshan.\nOn which Indra constantly rains. The king, who is not able to understand the words of the Guru. Dadah girimedhobhih samantatagnimutsrijan. 11. Lexicon"
